# tunnel-puck settings for one puck. Make yours with scripts/tpk-ctl.sh new (or copy
# this file to configs/<name>.conf; git ignores that name) and fill it in. The
# name is the puck's: up to 10 of a-z, 0-9 and -, starting with a letter, e.g.
# configs/home.conf. Shell syntax: NAME="value". scripts/tpk-ctl.sh reads it on
# your computer: tpk-ctl.sh <name> <command> (the name can be left out while
# there's only one). No secrets go in here: the Cloudflare token stays in its own
# file.

# --- The puck ------------------------------------------------------------------
# tpk-ctl.sh reaches the puck the first way that answers, and says which it used:
#   1. SSH_LAN, on your home network while you build it
#   2. through the VPS over the WireGuard link, the everyday way after drop-off
#   3. SSH_CLOUDFLARE, the emergency door (below), when the link is down
SSH_LAN="pi@tunnel-puck.local"      # user@name of the puck on your home network (the user and hostname set when flashing it)

# Optional: the emergency door. SSH through a Cloudflare Tunnel, which doesn't
# depend on the WireGuard link, so you can still get in and fix the puck when the
# link is down (a blocked port, a changed VPS IP, a bad change). Costs a domain
# on Cloudflare and step 1.3. Empty: no Cloudflare; if the link is down after
# drop-off, the puck must be power-cycled, or brought home.
SSH_CLOUDFLARE=""                   # e.g. ssh.puck.example.com, a hostname on your Cloudflare domain
CF_TOKEN_FILE="$HOME/.config/tunnel-puck/cloudflared.token"    # with SSH_CLOUDFLARE: the tunnel token alone, mode 600

# --- The VPS and routing -------------------------------------------------------
# What sends clients into the puck:
#   tpk: tunnel-puck's own rules, on any WireGuard VPS. Fill in the tpk block.
#   wgt: the VPS runs wgt (the wireguard-tools in this repository); the puck
#        becomes a wgt jump, and the VPS is driven through wgt-ctl. Fill in the
#        wgt block.
TPK_ROUTING="tpk"

PUCK_PORT=""                        # the link's UDP port on the VPS. Empty: link picks a free one (from 51821) and saves it here. 443, if free, is rarely filtered
# The link's subnet on the VPS, a /30 in 10.99.0.0/24. Leave it empty: link picks
# one nothing on the VPS uses and saves it here. Never change it once set.
TPK_LINK=""

# TPK_ROUTING="tpk" only
VPS_SSH="root@vps.example.com"      # how ssh reaches the VPS: root, or a user with passwordless sudo
VPS_HOST="vps.example.com"          # the VPS's public name or IP, as the puck dials it
CLIENT_IPS=""                       # the clients that exit via the puck: space-separated IPv4 addresses, as in each client's config
CLIENT_IP6S=""                      # their IPv6 addresses, if any (blocked: the link is IPv4 only)
WG_IF="wg0"                         # the WireGuard interface those clients connect to on the VPS
WG_SUBNET=""                        # leave empty (it means every subnet WG_IF is on)

# TPK_ROUTING="wgt" only
# The VPS's name in wgt-ctl's list (`bin/wgt-ctl list`). Its ssh target and
# address come from that list. Empty: tpk-ctl.sh asks, offering the listed
# servers or a new one (bin/wgt-ctl init), and saves the answer here.
WGT_SERVER=""
# Jump codes are wgt's: 2-6 of a-z 0-9 -. Empty, they're made from the puck's
# name, shortened to fit (grandma: grandm, LAN gran-l); tpk-ctl.sh config shows
# them. A code the VPS already uses for something else is refused.
JUMP_CODE=""                        # the puck's jump on the VPS; empty: the puck's name (6 at most)
JUMP_LABEL=""                       # a label for it, e.g. "Friend's house"; empty: the puck's name
JUMP_LAN=""                         # yes: a second jump in LAN mode (2.3), its code the jump's with -l (mom: mom-l)
JUMP_LAN_CODE=""                    # or name the LAN jump yourself (this also turns it on)
JUMP_LAN_LABEL=""                   # empty: the jump's label with " (LAN)"
